            <ArticleTitle>Low serum testosterone and increased mortality in men with coronary heart disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1821-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/hrt.2010.195412</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">To examine the effect of serum testosterone levels on survival in a consecutive series of men with confirmed coronary disease and calculate the prevalence of testosterone deficiency.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Longitudinal follow-up study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Tertiary referral cardiothoracic centre. Patients 930 consecutive men with coronary disease referred for diagnostic angiography recruited between June 2000 and June 2002 and followed up for a mean of 6.9±2.1 years.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OUTCOME" NlmCategory="RESULTS">All-cause mortality and vascular mortality. Prevalence of testosterone deficiency.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The overall prevalence of biochemical testosterone deficiency in the coronary disease cohort using bio-available testosterone (bio-T) &lt;2.6 nmol/l was 20.9%, using total testosterone &lt;8.1 nmol/l was 16.9% and using either was 24%. Excess mortality was noted in the androgen-deficient group compared with normal (41 (21%) vs 88 (12%), p=0.002). The only parameters found to influence time to all-cause and vascular mortality (HR ± 95% CI) in multivariate analyses were the presence of left ventricular dysfunction (3.85; 1.72 to 8.33), aspirin therapy (0.63; 0.38 to 1.0), β-blocker therapy (0.45; 0.31 to 0.67) and low serum bio-T (2.27; 1.45 to 3.6).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In patients with coronary disease testosterone deficiency is common and impacts significantly negatively on survival. Prospective trials of testosterone replacement are needed to assess the effect of treatment on survival.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
